taking the lead at a later point. And if the Landlord doesn't have a 2, I might still win.\n\nSo, I think I'll pass. It's the best move. I have a chance if the Landlord plays a big card to win the next trick. If I step on my teammate's card, I just ruin their round. The team's best bet is to let my teammate lead.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The teammate (Next Player) has played an Ace, a very high card, likely intended to seize the initiative or test the Landlord. The Landlord (Previous Player) has passed. I hold three 2s (♠2, ♥2, ♦2) and three unconnected singles (7, 8, 9). While I can beat the Ace with a 2, doing so would be a strategic error (often called "beating your own teammate"). It would waste my high control card and force me to lead one of my weak singles (7, 8, 9), which the Landlord could easily beat with a 10, K, or the remaining 2/Jokers, allowing the Landlord to regain control. By passing, I allow my teammate to win the trick and lead their next pattern (pair, triple, or straight), which suits their hand better.\n\nReason: My teammate is currently winning the trick after the Landlord passed. Interrupting their play by beating their Ace with a 2 would force me to lead weak singles, giving the advantage back to the Landlord. I should preserve my 2s to defend against the Landlord later.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": []}'}
